<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<odoo>

    <!-- THE SAPIAN MARK, inline and in its own four colours.

         Inline SVG rather than <img src="...">, because an <img> renders the
         SVG in a document of its own and cannot participate in the page's own
         sizing and accessibility. Inlined, the mark is part of the document.

         FOUR-COLOUR, AND THAT REVERSES A DECISION THIS FILE USED TO ARGUE FOR.

         Until 20 Aug 2026 the four petals carried `fill="currentColor"` and the
         mark took the colour of whatever enclosed it. The argument was that a
         1em glyph beside a line of text is a monochrome use, and that keeping
         the paint out of the markup kept re-branding to one edit.

         What that argument missed: the mark is not only ever a 1em glyph. It is
         the app-rail brand at 28px and the login-page mark at 48px, and at both
         of those sizes the four colours are legible and are what the product's
         identity actually is. Rendering the corporate mark in one flat colour
         everywhere meant the product never showed its own identity anywhere —
         and the operator saw exactly that.

         The paint now comes from `brand/sapian-logo.svg`, which is the ONE
         source: both the `d` attributes and the `fill` attributes below are
         byte-for-byte from that file. `tests_fast/test_sapian_mark_is_the_logo.py`
         asserts both, for this template and for the OWL copy in
         static/src/xml/app_rail.xml, so neither shape nor colour can drift and
         a redrawn mark cannot creep in — brand/README.md: "Never recreate or
         trace them."

         Re-branding is therefore still one edit; the edit is the SVG.

         LEGIBILITY. The mark is placed on the navbar, the login card and the
         app rail. All three are light surfaces in this theme, and the darkest
         petal is the brand teal, which is the same value the surrounding text
         already uses — so nothing here needs the monochrome treatment. A
         placement on a dark or brand-coloured surface WOULD: the teal petal
         would disappear into it. There is no such placement today, and
         `test_the_mark_sits_on_light_surfaces_only` fails if one appears.
    -->

    <template id="mark" name="Sapian mark (inline, four-colour)">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true"
             focusable="false" class="o_sapian_mark"
             xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
